# The display stack, carried in the initramfs so the panel comes up during it rather
# than after it. A second modules.d drop-in, not an edit of the SoC layer's `veyron`
# list: mkinitramfs reads every file in this directory and concatenates them, so a
# board adds to the family's list by shipping its own.
#
# This is a device-layer file for the same reason `kpart_size` is. The signed payload
# shares one fixed budget between the kernel image and the initramfs, and only a board
# whose firmware buffers 32 MiB has room for a display stack inside it — so the profile,
# the slot, and this list are one decision, made once, in one place.
#
# What it buys: on a board without it nothing can draw until the real root is mounted
# and udev loads these same modules, so a boot is a blank firmware screen from Ctrl+U
# until systemd is well underway, and an initramfs that *fails* is a blank screen
# forever. With it the panel lights during the initramfs, which is both a faster-looking
# boot and the difference between a diagnosable failure and a silent one.
#
# Names are the .ko basenames from the kernel's modules.order. Only drivers that bind a
# device-tree node are listed: initramfs-tools resolves symbol dependencies itself, so
# rockchipdrm pulls analogix_dp (the eDP bridge, which its built-in Rockchip glue calls
# into), drm, drm_kms_helper, drm_display_helper and drm_client_lib along with it.

# The display controller and its eDP output. CONFIG_DRM_FBDEV_EMULATION gives the
# console a framebuffer to land on, so loading this is what makes the panel show text.
rockchipdrm

# The panel itself — rk3288-veyron-edp.dtsi declares an `innolux,n116bge`, which is a
# panel-simple entry rather than a driver of its own.
panel-simple

# Backlight. Without these the panel is driven and black, which looks exactly like a
# panel that is not driven at all. The DT wires a `pwm-backlight` (pwm_bl) to `&pwm0`
# on the SoC's PWM controller (pwm-rockchip), and both ends have to be present. Their
# supplies are `regulator-fixed`, which is built in, and the RK808 rails feeding those
# are already in the SoC layer's list.
pwm_bl
pwm-rockchip
